insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-tie-to-shift', 1, 'What does applying a bulk shift of 8 ms do to a synthetic trace?', '["It stretches the trace so that the deeper events move further down the section than the shallow ones.","It rebuilds the synthetic with a wavelet delayed by 8 ms, which changes the shape of every event.","It adds 8 ms to the two way time of every sample, so the whole trace moves later together while the spacing between its events, their relative amplitudes and the shape of the wavelet stay exactly as they were.","It moves only the events that fall inside the correlation window."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A bulk shift is one constant added to every sample time, so it changes position and nothing internal. Reading it as a stretch confuses a bulk shift with a change to the time depth function.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-tie-to-shift', 2, 'Which situation does a bulk shift remove completely?', '["The seismic volume and the time depth function are referenced to datums that sit at different elevations.","The density log is washed out across part of the logged interval, so the synthetic carries an event that the observed seismic does not contain anywhere.","The processed data carries a phase rotation while the synthetic wavelet is zero phase.","The sonic reads too fast throughout the logged section."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A datum difference happens once, above the logged interval, so every sample inherits the same constant error and one constant removes it. The washed out density case is the classic error of shifting a synthetic that is wrong before it is moved.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-tie-to-shift', 3, 'Take a counterfactual in which the velocity assumed for the interval above the logs is 5 per cent too low. What can a bulk shift do about that error?', '["It removes the error near the top of the logged interval only, because the misfit grows steadily with two way time and no single constant can follow it downward.","It cannot help at all, because a velocity error above the logged interval changes the shape of each event as well as its position on the section.","It doubles the error.","It removes the error completely, because a discrepancy confined to the overburden is a constant inherited by every sample below it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'An error made once above the logs is a constant, which is exactly what a bulk shift corrects. The longest option applies stretch reasoning to an overburden error, which is the classic mix up between where an error is made and where it is seen.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-tie-to-shift', 4, 'The sonic reads systematically too fast throughout the logged section. What does the scan''s single suggested shift achieve?', '["It removes the misfit completely, because the correlation scan finds the single constant that makes the two traces agree over the whole logged interval.","It can balance the misfit but cannot remove it, because the misfit grows with two way time.","It removes the misfit at the base of the logged interval while worsening it at the top by the same amount.","It reports no shift at all, because the scan cannot score a pair of traces whose events are unevenly spaced."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A sonic error inside the logged interval produces a stretch, whose misfit is a function of time rather than a constant. Accepting a bulk shift as a cure for a stretch is the classic error here, and the fix is to adjust the time depth function itself.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-tie-to-shift', 5, 'The processed data carries a 90 degree phase rotation while your synthetic wavelet is zero phase. Why does no bulk shift fix the tie?', '["The correlation scan refuses to score any lag when the two wavelets differ in phase.","A phase rotation is just a time shift under another name, so the scan has already absorbed it into the 8 ms it reported.","The scan reports a negative correlation, which the winner rule discards.","The events differ in shape rather than in position, so a peak in one trace can correspond to a trough or a zero crossing in the other, and moving the trace never changes the shape of the wavelet in it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Phase is a property of the wavelet shape, and a bulk shift only relocates a trace. Moving the synthetic can bring one event into apparent agreement while making others worse, which is why the correlation stays mediocre wherever you put it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-tie-to-shift', 6, 'Why is it a strength rather than only a limitation that a bulk shift has exactly one degree of freedom?', '["A one parameter correction is easy to record, easy to reverse and easy for the next person to see, and it cannot manufacture agreement that the data does not contain.","It guarantees that the shift the scan returns is the true time depth error at the well, since with a single quantity to estimate there is nothing else left for the measurement to be wrong about.","It makes the correction independent of the wavelet used to build the synthetic.","It allows the scan to run faster than a fit with several parameters."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Corrections with many degrees of freedom can be tuned until any synthetic matches any seismic, at which point the match stops being evidence. One knob keeps the correction auditable and keeps the agreement meaningful.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-tie-to-shift', 7, 'You compare the synthetic against the observed trace and find that the misfit is roughly the same size and the same sign at the top, the middle and the base of the logged interval. What does that pattern indicate?', '["A stretch is present, so the time depth function needs adjusting rather than the trace needs moving.","A bulk shift is the right tool, because a misfit that does not vary with time is a constant.","The wavelet phase is wrong, because a constant misfit is the signature of a rotation applied to the whole trace by processing.","The synthetic contains an event that the observed data does not."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The shape of the residual misfit is the diagnostic: constant means shift, growing with time means stretch, and changing character between events means phase. Reaching for a shift when the misfit is not constant is how a tie gets forced.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-tie-to-shift', 8, 'Why does this tier run its scan on a case whose answer was planted in advance?', '["Because a planted lag makes the correlation easier to compute by hand.","Because real seismic cannot be correlated with a synthetic until a shift has been applied.","A scan always returns a lag and a number whatever it is given, so the only way to check that the sign convention, the search window, the sample stepping and your reading of the output are all correct is to run it where the answer is already known.","Because the engine needs a training case before it can score real data."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A method that never refuses to answer cannot be judged by whether it answered. On data with no known answer a wrong result looks exactly like a right one, so the four ways of being wrong stay invisible.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-tie-to-shift', 9, 'How is the observed trace in this tier constructed?', '["The 25 Hz synthetic is copied forward by four samples, which is 8 ms of two way time.","A 25 Hz synthetic and a 40 Hz synthetic are summed, and the result is delayed by four samples.","A trace is extracted from a processed seismic volume at the well location and resampled onto the 2 ms grid.","The synthetic is convolved a second time with the same wavelet, which delays it by half a wavelet length."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The observed trace is the synthetic and nothing else, moved 8 ms later in two way time, with the first four samples left as gaps. Nothing is added and nothing is removed, which is what makes the correct answer known before the scan runs.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-tie-to-shift', 10, 'Which result from this exercise should be carried forward as an expectation about real well ties?', '["That a correctly aligned tie returns a correlation of 1, so any tie scoring well below 1 should be treated as a failure and rebuilt from the logs.","That the correlation curve of a real tie is symmetric about the winning lag.","That the observed trace differs from the synthetic only by a time shift.","That a lag of zero, meaning a tie you never shifted, can still score a correlation of 0.621742 while sitting a full 8 ms wrong in two way time."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The mechanics and the reading habits transfer, and the headline value of 1 does not. Treating that 1 as the target is the classic error, because it makes every honest tie look broken.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-tie-to-shift', 11, 'Why does placing a synthetic require two numbers rather than one?', '["Because the scan reports its lag both in samples and in milliseconds, and a tie record is expected to carry the number in both of those forms.","The shift says what was done and says nothing about whether it was worth doing, so a quality number is needed as well.","Because a tie needs one shift for the top of the logged interval and a second shift for its base, and both have to be recorded.","Because the synthetic and the observed trace have different sample rates."]'::jsonb, 1, 'An alignment without a quality attached is an opinion formed at a display. Two interpreters running the same scan get the same pair of numbers and can then argue about the evidence rather than about the picture.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-tie-to-shift', 12, 'Why should a strongest amplitude read off a synthetic always be recorded together with the wavelet frequency that produced it?', '["Because that amplitude belongs to the wavelet as much as to the rock.","Because the engine cannot rebuild the synthetic unless the frequency is recorded with it.","Because a synthetic amplitude is quoted in units whose definition depends on the wavelet frequency.","Because the graded tolerance on the amplitude changes with the frequency at which it was read."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nothing about the rock changes between two runs at different frequencies, yet the strongest amplitude does. An amplitude quoted without its bandwidth is a joint statement about the geology and the wavelet with half of it missing.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-tie-to-shift', 13, 'The two graded peak times are given with a tolerance of 2 ms. What does that tolerance amount to on this dataset?', '["Two samples, since a tolerance of 2 ms is applied in each direction on a grid whose samples are spaced 2 ms apart.","A window loose enough to accept any peak that happens to fall within the same seismic event as the graded one.","One sample, since the grid is 2 ms, so the answer has to be the right sample rather than a neighbouring one.","A tenth of the dominant period of the 25 Hz wavelet."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The sample rate is 2 ms, so a 2 ms tolerance is exactly one sample. Reading it as a generous window is the classic error, because the graded peak time is a sample index rather than a region of the trace.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-tie-to-shift', 14, 'Why should a shift be written down as 8 ms of two way time rather than as 8?', '["Because the engine will reject a shift submitted without a unit, and the capstone marks an unlabelled answer wrong however close its value happens to be.","Because a bare number of that size could be metres, milliseconds of one way time or milliseconds of two way time, and only the label separates them.","Because the grading tolerance is expressed in two way time.","Because the scan reports its lags in samples."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Units and reference are part of the measurement rather than decoration on it. A shift recorded without them is a number waiting to be applied in the wrong domain months later.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-tie-to-shift', 15, 'Which activity belongs to the Expert tier rather than to this one?', '["Measuring a bulk shift by scanning lags and choosing the best correlation.","Comparing the strongest synthetic amplitude at two different wavelet frequencies.","Reading the correlation at zero lag.","Thinning a layer until the reflections from its top and its base merge, in order to find where the amplitude peaks."]'::jsonb, 3, 'This tier places the synthetic and reads the wavelet''s fingerprint on it. Thinning a layer to find where amplitude peaks is the next tier''s work, and so is anything that varies the angle of incidence.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-correlation-scan', 1, 'Why is the raw sum of sample by sample products not an adequate measure of how well two traces agree?', '["It cannot penalise a polarity reversal, because two negative samples multiply to give a positive product.","It grows with the size of the traces as well as with their agreement.","It ignores every sample where either trace happens to be near zero, so quiet intervals never contribute.","It can be computed at zero lag only, since a shifted comparison would need a normaliser."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Multiplying the observed trace by ten multiplies the sum by ten while nothing about the alignment changes. A measure built on it would report where the seismic is loudest rather than where it looks like the synthetic.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-correlation-scan', 2, 'Which behaviour does the raw sum of products already get right?', '["It returns the same value whatever gain has been applied to either of the two traces before the comparison.","It is bounded between minus one and plus one, so its value can be read the same way on any pair of traces.","It ignores gaps automatically.","A matched trough counts as much as a matched peak, since two negative samples give a positive product."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The product of samples rewards agreement in sign and size, which is why it sits at the heart of every version of this measure. What it lacks is a fixed scale, and normalisation supplies that.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-correlation-scan', 3, 'Using the teaching series s = [1, -2, 1] and g = [2, -4, 2], the raw sum of products is 12. Multiplying g by ten to give [20, -40, 20] makes it 120. What does that pair of results demonstrate?', '["That the raw sum reports the gain applied to a trace rather than the quality of the agreement, since the two series have exactly the same shape in both cases.","That the two series disagree more strongly after scaling, since every product in the sum has grown while neither of the series changed its position on the time axis.","That the raw sum is bounded once the traces are long enough.","That scaling a trace changes its polarity."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The shapes are identical before and after scaling, so any honest measure of agreement must return the same value. The tenfold change in the sum is the scale problem in miniature.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-correlation-scan', 4, 'For the teaching series s = [1, -2, 1] and its inverted copy h = [-1, 2, -1], the normalised correlation is exactly minus one. What should that tell an interpreter?', '["The scan should select this lag, since its correlation has the largest magnitude anywhere in the search and therefore shows the strongest agreement.","The synthetic needs shifting by half of the dominant period, so that its peaks come to land on the observed troughs.","The two traces have the same shape with opposite sign, which no amount of moving the trace will fix.","The two traces have nothing in common, so the comparison carries no information."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A value of minus one means identical shape and opposite sign, which points at a polarity convention rather than at an alignment. Selecting it because its magnitude is largest is the classic error the winner rule is designed to prevent.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-correlation-scan', 5, 'What is the normaliser in the engine''s correlation, and why does it work?', '["The square root of the product of the two sums of squares, because a gain applied to either trace multiplies the numerator and that square root by the same factor, so the two cancel.","The number of samples counted at that lag, which removes the effect of overlap length.","The largest absolute sample on each trace, which sets both peaks to one.","The mean of each trace, which removes any constant offset."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Squaring turns a gain of k into a factor of k squared, and the square root turns it back into k. The measure is then blind to the gain on either trace and sensitive only to shape.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-correlation-scan', 6, 'The engine takes its sums of squares about zero rather than about each trace''s mean. Why does that suit seismic traces?', '["Because subtracting a mean would make the measure sensitive to the gain applied to each trace before comparison.","Because the mean of a trace cannot be computed at all when gaps are present among its samples.","Because the result would then no longer be bounded between minus one and plus one.","Both traces already oscillate about zero, so there is no baseline to remove."]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a correlation about zero rather than a statistical correlation coefficient about a mean, and the choice is deliberate. A trace carrying a genuine constant offset has a defect that should be seen rather than quietly removed.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-correlation-scan', 7, 'A sample where either trace has no value is excluded from all three sums rather than treated as a zero. Why does that distinction matter?', '["Because a sample recorded as zero would make the denominator vanish and leave the correlation undefined, so the engine could not score that lag at all, whatever the rest of the trace looked like.","Treating a gap as zero would add a term of zero to the numerator and to both sums of squares, diluting the correlation with samples that carry no information.","Because gaps are stored as very large numbers that would dominate both sums.","Because the engine cannot multiply a gap."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A gap is a declared non value rather than a measurement of zero, and the platform keeps those distinct. Counting gaps as zeros lowers a correlation for a reason that has nothing to do with alignment.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-correlation-scan', 8, 'The engine refuses to score any lag with fewer than eight live overlapping samples. What is that rule guarding against?', '["An overlap shorter than the wavelet, which would bias the scored correlation toward the low frequency end of the band.","A division by zero at any lag where one of the two traces happens to be completely flat.","The extra arithmetic cost of scoring lags at the far ends of the scan.","A correlation computed on two or three samples can reach a large value by accident."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The far ends of the scan are where the two traces barely overlap, and a high score from a handful of samples is not evidence. The engine returns no value there rather than a value that could be mistaken for a result.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-correlation-scan', 9, 'Why must the sums of squares be recomputed at every lag rather than computed once per trace and reused?', '["Because the sample rate changes with the lag.","Because the traces are rescaled at each lag before comparison.","Because the set of counted positions changes with the lag, as samples move in and out of the overlap and gaps in one trace exclude different positions in the other as it slides.","Because the engine subtracts a fresh mean at each lag."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The correlation at each lag is computed over its own set of live overlapping positions, so all three sums belong to that set. Reusing a whole trace energy would mix samples that were never compared into the normaliser.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-correlation-scan', 10, 'What does the bound on the normalised correlation guarantee, and where does it come from?', '["The value can never leave the range from minus one to plus one, which follows from the Cauchy Schwarz inequality.","That the value grows with the number of samples counted at the lag, so a longer overlap always scores higher than a short one.","That the value is always positive, since squares appear in the denominator.","That the value is independent of the sample rate."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The inequality says that a sum of products cannot exceed the square root of the product of the two sums of squares. That bound is what makes the number readable on any pair of traces, at any lag, in any project.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-correlation-scan', 11, 'The scan in this exercise uses a window of 40 ms on a 2 ms sample grid. How many lags does it test?', '["40 lags, being 20 negative lags and 20 positive lags across the window.","41 lags, covering minus 40 ms to plus 40 ms in steps of 2 ms.","20 lags, since the window of 40 ms divided by the 2 ms step gives 20 steps.","81 lags, since every millisecond between minus 40 and plus 40 is tested along with zero."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Forty milliseconds is 20 samples at 2 ms, so the search runs from lag minus 20 to lag plus 20 in whole samples. That is 20 negative lags, 20 positive lags and zero lag itself.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-correlation-scan', 12, 'The scan steps in whole samples and never tests a fraction of a sample. What follows for the shift it reports?', '["The reported shift is always positive, because fractional lags would be needed to express a negative one.","The reported shift is accurate to better than one sample, because the peak is interpolated.","The reported shift is always a whole number of samples, so on this 2 ms grid it is always an even number of ms and a value such as 7 ms could never be returned.","The reported shift is quoted in samples rather than in ms."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Testing a fractional lag would mean interpolating the trace, and the engine will not invent samples that were never recorded. The resolution of the answer is the sample rate, which is why the graded tolerance of 0.5 ms sits comfortably inside one sample.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-correlation-scan', 13, 'Among all the lags it scored, how does the scan choose its winner?', '["It takes the lag with the largest absolute correlation, so that a strongly negative value can win when it is the most extreme in the search.","It takes the lag closest to zero among those above a fixed correlation threshold.","It takes the midpoint of the lags whose correlation exceeds the average of the scan.","It takes the lag with the highest correlation."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Highest and largest in magnitude are different rules, and the engine uses the first. Selecting on magnitude is the classic error, because it lets a polarity reversal be reported as a tie.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-correlation-scan', 14, 'The engine pairs synthetic sample i with observed sample i plus the lag. Given that construction, why is the answer plus 8 ms rather than minus 8 ms?', '["Because the correlation curve is symmetric about zero lag, so of the two equally good answers the engine reports the positive one, which is the house convention.","Observed sample i plus four holds the value of synthetic sample i, so the matching pairing is the one with a lag of plus four samples, which is plus 8 ms.","Because a negative lag would place the synthetic outside the search window.","Because the scan discards negative lags before choosing a winner."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sign falls out of the pairing rule and can be derived rather than memorised. At a lag of minus four the synthetic is compared with a copy of itself 16 ms away, which is not identical, so that lag does not win.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-correlation-scan', 15, 'Write the reason the correlation at the winning lag is exactly 1 in this exercise.', '["Because the observed trace was rescaled to the same amplitude range as the synthetic before the comparison was made, which removes every difference between them.","Because the wavelet used to build the synthetic is zero phase, which lets it correlate perfectly with any trace built from the same reflectivity series.","At that lag the observed sample equals the synthetic sample at every counted position, so the numerator and the denominator are the same quantity.","Because 41 lags is enough for the scan to find a perfect match."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The cancellation is arithmetic and involves no property of the rock, the wavelet or the sample rate. Correlating a series with a shifted copy of itself is an autocorrelation, and it returns 1 for any trace whatever.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-reading-the-scan-curve', 1, 'The scan curve scores 0.972386 at both 6 ms and 10 ms, 0.892968 at both 4 ms and 12 ms, and 0.621742 at both 0 ms and 16 ms. What accounts for that symmetry about 8 ms?', '["Normalisation of the correlation forces every scan curve to be symmetric about the lag it eventually selects as the best one, whatever the data underneath it happens to look like.","The observed trace here is the synthetic copied forward, so the scan is an autocorrelation, which depends only on how far a lag sits from alignment.","The Ricker wavelet is symmetric in time, so the correlation curve inherits that symmetry.","The search window runs from -40 ms to +40 ms, which is itself symmetric."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sliding a trace against a shifted copy of itself is an autocorrelation, and an autocorrelation is an even function of lag, so being 2 ms early costs exactly what being 2 ms late costs. The classic error is the first option, which treats normalisation as though it imposed a shape on the curve rather than putting different lags on a common scale.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-reading-the-scan-curve', 2, 'The best lag on this scan is 8 ms. Which other lag scores exactly the same correlation as a lag of 4 ms?', '["A lag of -4 ms, because the curve is symmetric about zero lag rather than about the answer.","A lag of 16 ms, because it lies twice as far from the answer as 4 ms does.","No other lag, because each lag in the scan produces its own distinct correlation.","A lag of 12 ms, which sits 4 ms from the answer on the other side."]'::jsonb, 3, '4 ms sits 4 ms below the answer and 12 ms sits 4 ms above it, so both score 0.892968. The classic error is the first option, which measures the symmetry from zero lag instead of from the peak.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-reading-the-scan-curve', 3, 'At a 2 ms sample rate with a search window of 40 ms either side, what does the scan actually produce?', '["A correlation at each of 41 lags from -40 ms to +40 ms, of which the best lag and its correlation are reported while the rest form the curve.","A single correlation computed at the lag the interpreter has already chosen to apply.","A correlation at every lag, each one weighted by the number of samples that overlapped.","Forty one correlations, from which the scan returns the average as the recommended shift."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The function walks every lag in one sample steps, scores each over the live overlapping samples, and returns the best lag together with the full series. Reading only the winning number discards the curve, which is where the evidence about precision and about competing lags lives.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-reading-the-scan-curve', 4, 'On this exercise the scan reports a correlation of 0.621742 at a lag of 0 ms. What does that number describe?', '["The residual disagreement that remains after the recommended bulk shift of 8 ms has been applied to the synthetic.","The lowest correlation the scan encountered anywhere inside its search window of 40 ms either side.","How well the two traces resemble each other with no shift applied, on a pair that is 8 ms out.","The correlation that would be obtained if the synthetic had been built at a different wavelet frequency entirely."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A lag of 0 ms means nothing was moved, so 0.621742 measures resemblance in whatever position the traces happened to be in while the pair is a full 8 ms out of alignment. It is also nowhere near the bottom of the range, since the worst correlation in this scan is -0.409277.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-reading-the-scan-curve', 5, 'A colleague ties a well, applies no shift, and offers a correlation of 0.621742 as evidence that the tie is good. What is wrong with the reasoning?', '["Nothing is wrong, since a correlation above a half means most of the shape of the two traces agrees.","The number is too low to report at all, and a tie should be rejected whenever its correlation falls below the peak the scan found.","The correlation should have been computed over the whole trace rather than over the live overlapping samples only.","A correlation measures resemblance at the position the traces are in, and only a correlation compared against every other lag in the window shows that the position is the best one available."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A plausible correlation is not evidence of alignment, and this pair scores 0.621742 while sitting a full 8 ms wrong. The classic error is the first option, accepting a respectable looking number as proof that nothing needs shifting.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-reading-the-scan-curve', 6, 'Which statement correctly separates what a correlation tells you from what it does not?', '["A correlation above a half tells you the traces are aligned, because misalignment would drive the value towards zero long before it ever reached that level.","A high correlation at your chosen shift shows resemblance at that shift, and only a high correlation relative to every other shift shows alignment.","A correlation is a measure of amplitude agreement, so it reports the strength of the reflections rather than where they sit in time.","A correlation tells you nothing at all until a bulk shift has been applied."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A shift is right or wrong only by comparison with the alternatives, which is why the scan tests a window rather than scoring one lag. The classic error is the first option, reading a comfortable value as proof of position.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-reading-the-scan-curve', 7, 'Why does a misalignment of 8 ms still leave the correlation as high as 0.621742 on this noiseless pair?', '["Because the normalised correlation divides out the energy in both traces, and that removes most of the effect a time shift would otherwise have on the number.","Because the scan counts only samples that overlap and are live, and a shift of 8 ms removes very few of them.","Because seismic traces are oscillatory and smooth, and 8 ms is about a fifth of the 40 ms dominant period of a 25 Hz wavelet, so peaks still sit largely on peaks.","Because the correlation is computed over the whole logged interval, so a local error is diluted."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The very smoothness that makes traces easy to look at is what stops misalignment from looking bad, since a fifth of a period of slide leaves peaks mostly on peaks. Normalisation puts lags on a common scale and does not protect the number from a shift.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-reading-the-scan-curve', 8, 'One sample either side of the answer the correlation is still 0.972386. What does that broad shoulder tell you?', '["The best lag is well determined as a choice but only loosely constrained in value.","The scan has failed to discriminate between lags, so the shift it returned should be treated as unreliable until a sharper method is used.","The reflectivity series contains too few coefficients for the correlation to be decisive.","The observed trace must contain noise, since a clean pair would fall away much faster."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correctness and precision are different properties: the peak is exact, and the shoulders say the data would have been nearly as happy two samples away. The classic error is the second option, reading a broad peak as a bad result rather than as information about how tightly the shift is pinned down.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-reading-the-scan-curve', 9, 'By how much does one sample of misalignment reduce the correlation on this scan, and what does that quantity describe?', '["By 0.107032, which is the difference between the peak and the correlation at 4 ms, and it describes the width of the search window.","By 0.027614, and it describes how little the data penalises a shift that is 2 ms out.","By 0.378258, which is the drop all the way out to zero lag, and it describes the total range of the scan.","By nothing measurable, since adjacent lags on a symmetric curve score identically."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The peak is 1.000000 and both neighbours are 0.972386, so one sample costs 0.027614. The last option confuses symmetry, which pairs lags on opposite sides of the answer, with equality between a lag and the answer itself.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-reading-the-scan-curve', 10, 'On real data, what practical consequence follows from a scan peak whose neighbouring lags score almost as highly?', '["Noise can move the correlation by more than the gap between adjacent lags, so the last sample of the reported shift should not be treated as decisive.","The scan should be re-run with a wider search window and a finer lag step, because a broad peak is a sign that the true lag lies outside the range that was actually tested.","The correlation values should be rounded before they are compared with each other.","The shift can be quoted to a finer precision than the sample rate allows."]'::jsonb, 0, 'One sample of misalignment costs 0.027614 here, which is the sort of quantity noise on a real trace can overturn, so the ordering of adjacent lags is not gospel. Widening the window does not narrow a peak, since the width comes from how slowly the traces stop resembling each other.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-reading-the-scan-curve', 11, 'The synthetic is stretched relative to the seismic, so the interval between two events is longer on the synthetic than on the seismic. What does the scan report?', '["Nothing, because the correlation never reaches the minimum of 8 live overlapping samples.","A negative correlation, since a stretched synthetic cannot agree with the seismic in polarity.","Two peaks of equal height, one for the top of the interval and one for the base.","A single lag and a correlation that both look ordinary, because the scan can only express a constant shift and returns whichever constant shift leaves the least disagreement."]'::jsonb, 3, 'An algorithm that can report only one kind of answer reports that kind whatever the data was asking, so a stretch comes back looking like an ordinary result. The classic error is expecting a bulk shift to fix an interval that is the wrong length.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-reading-the-scan-curve', 12, 'Why can a bulk shift not correct a phase rotation between the synthetic and the seismic?', '["Because a phase rotation is too small an effect to matter at these sample rates, so the shift ends up applied to the wrong part of the section entirely.","Because the correlation used in the scan is normalised, which removes any information about phase before the lags are compared.","Because shifting moves an event in time while a rotation changes its shape.","Because a rotation alters the reflection coefficients themselves."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A shift and a rotation are different operations, so no lag undoes a rotation, and the shift returned mixes the real time error with the phase error under one unlabelled number. A rotation acts on the wavelet, so the reflection coefficients are untouched by it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-reading-the-scan-curve', 13, 'You tie a synthetic to a reflector you believed corresponds to a formation top, and that correspondence is wrong. What does the correlation tell you about the mistake?', '["Nothing, because the scan aligns the synthetic to whatever it was pointed at and holds no opinion about which reflector you meant.","The correlation drops far enough to expose the error, since tying to the wrong reflector puts every event in the synthetic in the wrong place at once.","The correlation becomes undefined, because the scan cannot score a tie without a marker.","The correlation is reported as zero until a valid marker is supplied."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A mis-picked marker is invisible to the scan, which measures agreement between traces and never learns which reflector you intended. The classic error is the second option, expecting the correlation to police a geological decision it has no access to.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-reading-the-scan-curve', 14, 'Which check in your own workflow is most likely to expose a stretch that the scan has hidden behind one acceptable looking shift?', '["Re-running the scan with a wider search window, so that the larger shift a stretched synthetic needs falls inside the range of lags being tested.","Correlating an upper window and a lower window separately and seeing whether they ask for different shifts.","Rebuilding the synthetic at a higher wavelet frequency so that the events become sharper and the scan peak narrows.","Reporting the correlation to more decimal places."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A stretch means the disagreement varies through the section, so two windows disagreeing about the shift is the direct evidence for it. A wider window still searches only constant shifts, so it cannot expose a varying one.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-reading-the-scan-curve', 15, 'The lowest correlation anywhere in this 41-lag scan is -0.409277. Why is that worth knowing when you quote a correlation?', '["Because a negative correlation anywhere in the scan means the polarity of the synthetic was reversed somewhere in processing and the whole tie has to be rebuilt.","Because the scan discards any lag scoring below zero before it selects the best one.","Because it shows how much room the scale has, so a correlation quoted on its own says where one lag landed without saying how the alternatives scored.","Because the average of the scan curve over all the lags tested is the quantity to report alongside the shift, rather than the value at the peak itself."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The scan explores everything from -0.409277 up to its peak and reports only the top of that range. A negative value at some lag is an ordinary consequence of peaks landing on troughs at that lag, not a statement about polarity in the data.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-tuning-moves-the-amplitude', 1, 'A synthetic seismogram has two inputs. Which of them is a measurement of the formation?', '["The reflectivity series, which comes from the sonic and density curves through impedance and contrast.","The wavelet, because its dominant frequency is chosen to match the bandwidth that the processing of this particular survey delivered.","Both of them, since the wavelet is estimated from the seismic while the reflectivity is estimated from the logs, so each one is measured.","Neither, since both are modelling choices."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every step from sonic and density to impedance to contrast is a measurement of the rock, while the wavelet is chosen and contributes no energy of its own. The classic error is the third option, which promotes an estimate made to match a processing outcome into a measurement of the earth.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-tuning-moves-the-amplitude', 2, 'Whose property is an amplitude on a synthetic trace?', '["The reflectivity''s, because the wavelet contributes no energy of its own and only scales what it is given.","The wavelet''s, because changing it changes the amplitude while the reflection coefficients stay fixed.","The pair''s, since both are needed to produce it.","The processing sequence''s, since that is what fixed the bandwidth of the volume."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The reflectivity fixes which contributions exist and how large each is, and the wavelet fixes how far each reaches and therefore which of them land on the same sample, so the amplitude belongs to the two together. The first two options are each half of the answer stated as though it were the whole of it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-tuning-moves-the-amplitude', 3, 'Between the 15 Hz run and the 40 Hz run on this well, what happens to the reflection coefficients?', '["Their sizes fall, because a shorter wavelet resolves each contrast more finely.","Nothing at all, because their count, their sizes, their signs and their times come from the sonic and density curves and no wavelet enters that arithmetic.","They move in time, because the wavelet sets where each contrast lands on the trace.","They are recomputed at each frequency from the impedance in the time domain."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The reflectivity is identical across all three runs, which is what makes the experiment a controlled one. The classic error is letting the wavelet reach backwards into the reflectivity, when in fact it only decides which coefficients end up sharing a sample.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-tuning-moves-the-amplitude', 4, 'Why do interpreters routinely forget that the wavelet is contributing to what they see?', '["Because the wavelet is removed during processing, after which a migrated volume shows reflection coefficients directly and there is nothing left for a wavelet to contribute.","Because the wavelet is much shorter in time than the interval being interpreted.","Because amplitude displays are normalised before they reach the interpreter.","Because it is the same everywhere on the display, so it never varies across the picture while everything that does vary looks like geology."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A quantity that is constant across a display becomes invisible, and the habit only breaks when the wavelet stops being constant, across a merge, a reprocessing, or between a synthetic and the seismic. The first option is the classic error, treating processing as though it delivered reflectivity rather than a band limited version of it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-tuning-moves-the-amplitude', 5, 'What is it about a wavelet that lets neighbouring reflection coefficients add together?', '["That its amplitude is normalised, so each coefficient contributes an equal share to the sum.","That its shape is symmetric, so contributions on either side of a coefficient are balanced.","That it has length, so each coefficient contributes to a span of samples.","That its dominant frequency sets the sign of each contribution it produces."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Length is the whole mechanism: a coefficient smears its scaled copy across a span of time, and wherever spans overlap the values add. Symmetry and normalisation describe the shape of the pulse and change nothing about whether neighbours can reach the same sample.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-tuning-moves-the-amplitude', 6, 'Why does lowering the dominant frequency raise the strongest amplitude on this well?', '["A longer wavelet reaches across more neighbouring coefficients, so more of them contribute to the same sample and where they share a sign they add to a larger total.","A lower frequency wavelet carries more energy, so every reflection it strikes returns a stronger response to the receiver.","A lower frequency wavelet has a taller central peak before it is convolved with anything.","Lower frequencies are attenuated less, so more of the signal survives to be recorded."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Wavelet length sets how many coefficients can reinforce at one sample, and nothing about the rock changed between the runs. The remaining options are all propagation stories, which cannot apply to a synthetic where no wave travelled anywhere.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-tuning-moves-the-amplitude', 7, 'How are resolution and amplitude related on this experiment?', '["They are independent properties of the trace, so a wavelet can be chosen that separates events and increases the amplitude of each one at the same time.","They rise together, because a sharper wavelet separates events more cleanly and also concentrates the energy of each one into a taller peak.","They are unrelated, since amplitude is set by the rock and resolution by the wavelet.","They are alternatives, because a wavelet that keeps neighbouring events separate has by that act stopped them reinforcing."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Separation and summing are the same fact seen from two sides, so the low frequency trace is brighter because it is worse at resolving. The second option is the classic error, the intuition that a sharper wavelet gives a bigger event.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-tuning-moves-the-amplitude', 8, 'The 25 Hz run is not graded in the capstone. What does including it add to the argument?', '["It supplies the capstone tolerance, since the graded values at 15 Hz and 40 Hz are each checked against the amplitude that the 25 Hz run produced.","It turns two points into a consistent trend in one direction, which is evidence of a mechanism rather than a coincidence of two endpoints.","It shows that the effect reverses somewhere in the middle of the frequency range.","It confirms that the sample rate of 2 ms is fine enough for all three wavelets."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A two point result could be an accident of where those two wavelets happened to land, while three points falling monotonically point to a mechanism. The capstone tolerances are 0.002 at 15 Hz and 0.001 at 40 Hz and have nothing to do with the middle run.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-tuning-moves-the-amplitude', 9, 'The strongest amplitude at 15 Hz is over four times the strongest amplitude at 40 Hz. What does that comparison establish?', '["That peak amplitude is not measuring the strength of any interface, because the strength of every interface in the well was held constant while the number moved by that factor.","That the 15 Hz wavelet is over four times longer in time than the 40 Hz wavelet.","That the well contains a reflector which is visible only at low frequency and disappears at higher frequencies.","That the reflection coefficients are over four times larger at 15 Hz."]'::jsonb, 0, 'One input varied and the output moved by a factor of over four, so whatever the peak is measuring, it is not reflector strength. The third and fourth options both smuggle a change in the earth into an experiment where the earth was held fixed.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-tuning-moves-the-amplitude', 10, 'A survey is reprocessed to a higher bandwidth and the amplitudes on a prospect drop. What is the sound conclusion?', '["That the reservoir is weaker than the older data suggested, since the newer processing resolves the interfaces more accurately and therefore measures their strength more honestly.","That the reprocessing has introduced an error somewhere, because the bandwidth chosen for a volume should have no bearing on the amplitude of any event recorded within it.","That the prospect should be re-ranked below the others on the new volume.","That the shorter wavelet has reduced the constructive summing between neighbouring reflections, so the two sets of amplitudes are not comparable."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Raising the bandwidth shortens the wavelet, which reduces how much neighbouring reflections reinforce, and nothing about the reservoir changed. The first option is the classic error, inferring reflector strength from amplitude across two different wavelets.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-tuning-moves-the-amplitude', 11, 'Sorting prospects by peak amplitude sorts them by how well each one suited the wavelet. What does that ordering fail to guarantee?', '["It fails to guarantee nothing at all, because peak amplitude is a direct measurement of contrast strength and any ordering built from it reproduces the ordering by contrast exactly.","That the prospects were mapped on the same horizon.","That the ordering matches the ordering by contrast strength, and nothing on the map tells you whether it does.","That the amplitudes were measured at the same time sample."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ordering may agree with the ordering by contrast, and the map carries no evidence either way, which is the reason to go back to a tied well. The first option is the classic error, treating amplitude as a direct read of reflector strength.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-tuning-moves-the-amplitude', 12, 'Which case does the lesson call the more expensive mistake, and why?', '["The bright case, because a bright event attracts attention and money before anyone has checked whether the brightness came from contrast or from summing.","The dim case, because a strong contrast cancelled by a neighbour draws no attention at all.","The bright case, because destructive interference is rarer than constructive interference on real data.","The dim case, because low amplitudes are recorded with less numerical precision than high ones are."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A bright anomaly at least invites you to go and look at it, while a cancelled event never asks for the visit. The fourth option offers the right verdict for a reason that has nothing to do with interference.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-tuning-moves-the-amplitude', 13, 'A bright event appears on a synthetic. What is it evidence of?', '["A large reflection coefficient at that time.","A thin bed at that time.","A change in the reflectivity between two runs.","That the contributions reaching that sample happened to reinforce, which can come from one large contrast, from several moderate contrasts spaced to add, or from a wavelet long enough to gather a package of layering into one sum."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Brightness is the size of a sum, and several different arrangements produce the same bright event, so the display alone cannot tell them apart. The first option is the classic error, reading a bright event as a strong reflector.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-tuning-moves-the-amplitude', 14, 'Why does separating amplitude from reflectivity require a tied well rather than the seismic alone?', '["Because the reflectivity series is the only thing in the workflow that belongs to the rock alone, and a tie is what puts it next to the trace.","Because a tie removes the wavelet from the seismic, after which the section shows reflection coefficients directly and no separation is needed any more.","Because a tie converts the seismic from time into depth, where amplitudes become comparable.","Because a tie increases the bandwidth of the volume."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Only the reflectivity is free of the wavelet, and a tie is what places it alongside the trace so bright events can be checked against the contrasts that produced them. A tie changes neither the wavelet nor the bandwidth of the seismic.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-tuning-moves-the-amplitude', 15, 'Which pair of statements is labelled correctly?', '["Both are observations about the trace, since brightness and reflector strength are two names for the same measured quantity on a migrated section.","Both are claims about the earth, since a trace is a record of the earth and everything read from it is therefore a statement about rock.","This event is bright is an observation about the trace, and this event is a strong reflector is a claim about the earth.","This event is bright is the claim about the earth, and the strong reflector wording is the observation."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Brightness is measurable and repeatable on a dataset, while reflector strength requires knowing what the wavelet contributed. The first option is the classic error, collapsing the two into one quantity.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-tuning-moves-the-time', 1, 'A report on the teaching well states that the strongest synthetic amplitude is 0.1573149710893631 at 1646 ms TWT. What is wrong with it, and why is the fault hard to catch?', '["The two-way time lies outside the interval the well supports.","Nothing is wrong with it, because both values are ones the engine reports for this well, and any reader who needs the frequency can recover it later from the project notes or by rerunning the panel themselves.","The two numbers were read at different frequencies, and each is genuine on its own, so no check on a single number flags the pairing.","The amplitude is impossible, because no synthetic peak can exceed the largest coefficient in the well."]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is the crossed pair, the characteristic failure of the reading task. Both halves are real numbers from this well, so only a check on the pair catches it, and the option that accepts the line is the classic error of treating a frequency as something that can be added back afterwards.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-tuning-moves-the-time', 2, 'Without looking anything up, what internal check tells you that a peak reading on the teaching well has been assembled from two different runs?', '["On this well the amplitude falls as the frequency rises while the peak moves later, so the largest amplitude belongs to the earliest time and a large amplitude quoted at a late time contradicts itself.","The reported time will sit outside the logged interval of the well, which is the range the panel statistics are computed over.","The amplitude will fail to match the strongest reflection coefficient in the well.","The amplitude will be reported as a negative number."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Amplitude and time move together when the frequency changes, so the pair carries an internal consistency you can test by eye. Checking either number on its own proves nothing, which is why the check is stated on the pair.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-tuning-moves-the-time', 3, 'Which four things have to travel together for a peak reading to be complete?', '["The amplitude, the two-way time, the depth of the interface it came from, and the name of the well whose logs the synthetic was built from.","The amplitude, the two-way time, the correlation of the tie and the bulk shift that was applied to the synthetic.","The amplitude, the sample rate of the grid, the dominant frequency and the logged interval.","The amplitude, the two-way time, the dominant frequency of the wavelet, and the fact that it is a synthetic."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Drop the frequency and no reader can compare your number with anyone else''s, and drop the word synthetic and a modelled response to one well''s logs can be taken for observed data. The other options swap one of the four for something the reader can already work out.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-tuning-moves-the-time', 4, 'Why does the reading procedure insist that you set the frequency first and take the amplitude and its two-way time in the same glance?', '["Because the engine recomputes the reflection coefficients whenever the frequency control moves, which leaves any reading taken in the other order stale by a sample or two.","Because reading the amplitudes first and collecting the times afterwards leaves two lists to be matched from memory, and the matching is where the error enters.","Because the panel clears the previous reading as soon as the frequency changes.","Because the capstone grades the amplitude and the time as one field."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The order exists to remove the moment when a number sits in your notes without the frequency and time it belongs to. The first option is wrong because the reflectivity is fixed before any wavelet is chosen.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-tuning-moves-the-time', 5, 'What makes a pick defensible in the sense this tier means?', '["It sits on the strongest event in the section, which is the choice every interpreter would independently arrive at and is therefore the most reproducible anchor available anywhere on the display.","It agrees with the picks made in the neighbouring wells of the same survey, which is the usual evidence that a horizon has been followed correctly.","It was made on the volume with the widest bandwidth available to the project.","It is anchored to a contrast you can name in the reflectivity series."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A defensible pick can be stated in terms of the well rather than in terms of the display, which is what lets somebody else reproduce it at another bandwidth. The longest option is the classic error of treating brightness as a reflector, and its appeal to agreement between interpreters is the consistency a shared bandwidth manufactures.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-tuning-moves-the-time', 6, 'Why is the 25 Hz reading the most available wrong answer in this capstone?', '["Because it is the pair you have worked with since the Associate tier while the capstone grades only the 15 Hz and 40 Hz readings, so it is familiar and inapplicable at the same time.","Because the observed trace in the exercise was built at 25 Hz, so the capstone deliberately excludes it from grading.","Because it is the only pair whose amplitude exceeds the strongest coefficient.","Because the engine reports it to fewer digits than the other two."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Familiarity is what makes it dangerous, since a number you have seen many times is the one most likely to be written from memory rather than read from the panel. The second option confuses the frequency the observed trace was built at with the frequencies the capstone grades.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-tuning-moves-the-time', 7, 'Why can a statement built only from peak readings not support a comparison?', '["Because the panel reports peaks only over the live window of the trace, so the comparison would rest on a masked interval.","Because a peak amplitude is a sum of overlapping contributions rather than a single reflection.","Because both coordinates of a peak belong to the wavelet you chose.","Because peak readings are dimensionless."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A comparison needs one end of it to hold still, and neither the amplitude nor the time of a peak does. The second option is true of a peak but does not explain why the comparison fails.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-tuning-moves-the-time', 8, 'Two seismic volumes cover the same field and were processed at different times to different bandwidths. Which quantity can be carried across the boundary between them?', '["The bulk shift measured on the older volume, since the well itself has not moved between the two surveys and the shift is a property of the well location.","The reflectivity computed from the well, which knows of neither survey.","The brightest event in each volume, since placing the two amplitudes side by side is what shows how the rock differs between the surveys and is the standard way of comparing vintages.","The peak amplitude, once scaled by the ratio of the two dominant frequencies."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The reflectivity is the common currency because it was fixed before any wavelet was chosen and is valid against both volumes. The longest option is the classic error of comparing amplitudes across surveys, and the last assumes a scaling relationship that does not exist.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-tuning-moves-the-time', 9, 'A prospect ranking is built from amplitudes taken from more than one seismic survey. What is the objection?', '["The surveys will not share a datum, so the amplitudes being ranked sit at different two-way times and the comparison is between features at different depths.","The ranking will be dominated by the largest prospect regardless of which survey it was measured on.","Amplitudes are dimensionless, so they cannot be ranked at all.","The amplitudes passed through different wavelets, so the ranking reflects the processing."]'::jsonb, 3, 'An amplitude belongs to the survey that produced it, so a table mixing surveys compares experiments as well as rocks. Building the ranking anyway is the classic error of comparing amplitudes across surveys.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-tuning-moves-the-time', 10, 'Partway through a tie the reflection coefficients are where you expect them but the amplitudes are not. Which half of the problem should you investigate?', '["The wavelet, the bandwidth and the interference between neighbouring coefficients, because the coefficients have already cleared the logs and re-reading the sonic would be wasted effort.","The sonic curve, starting with the unit declared for it in the file header.","The time-depth function, since the times attached to the events must be wrong.","The density curve over any washed-out interval."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The reflectivity splits a tie problem in two, because it is the last quantity in the chain that is still a statement about rock alone. The three shorter options all send you upstream to inputs the coefficients have already vindicated.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-tuning-moves-the-time', 11, 'You rebuild a synthetic on the same well and find the strongest reflection coefficient at a different two-way time from before, having changed nothing but the wavelet frequency. What have you found?', '["A data problem upstream in the curves or the time-depth function.","A tuning effect of exactly the kind this module describes.","A normal result, since the coefficients are recomputed at every frequency and their times follow the wavelet that produced them.","Evidence that the observed trace was misaligned."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The reflectivity is fixed before the wavelet is chosen, so a move in it is an alarm about the well data rather than a property of the experiment. The second option is the classic error of attributing anything that moved to tuning.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-tuning-moves-the-time', 12, 'How does the 66 ms swing in the peak time compare with the misalignment the bulk-shift scan corrects on this exercise?', '["It is about the same size as the 8 ms shift, so a scan run after the wavelet is chosen removes both effects together.","It is about a quarter of the shift, which is why the scan is able to absorb it.","It is about 8.25 times the 8 ms shift.","It is about twice the shift, which the scan is able to absorb."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sizing one effect against another you already understand is the point of the comparison, and 66 ms against 8 ms is a factor of 8.25. Treating the two as cancelling misreads them as the same kind of quantity.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-tuning-moves-the-time', 13, 'Two picks made on the brightest event at different bandwidths sit 66 ms apart. Why can a scan running from minus 40 ms to plus 40 ms not reconcile them?', '["The scan reports only its best lag and not the curve.","The scan steps in 2 ms, so 66 ms is not one of the lags it is able to test.","The scan requires at least eight live samples, which the gap does not leave.","The gap is wider than the whole one-sided range of the scan, and tuning movement and misalignment are different problems, so a shift scan is the wrong instrument for the first of them."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The scan is sized for the misalignment it exists to correct, so its range is a statement about that problem rather than about tuning. The second option is wrong because 66 ms is an even number of milliseconds and would be testable if the range reached it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-tuning-moves-the-time', 14, 'Which of the three frequencies on the teaching well would most easily teach a bad habit, and why?', '["40 Hz, because it puts the brightest event further from the largest contrast than any other frequency and therefore gives the most misleading picture of where the reflector actually sits.","25 Hz, because it is the frequency the observed trace was built at and so flatters the tie.","15 Hz, because brightness lands on the largest contrast there by coincidence of bandwidth, so the method gives the right answer for the wrong reason.","40 Hz, because its peak amplitude is the smallest of the three."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A method that returns the right answer for the wrong reason is worse than one that fails visibly, because nobody goes back and checks it. Reading 15 Hz as the good case and 40 Hz as the misleading one is the misconception this question exists to catch.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-tuning-moves-the-time', 15, 'Ten wells in one survey were all tied on the brightest event, and the ten ties agree with each other. What does that agreement establish?', '["That the ties are sound, since ten independent wells agreeing on the same surface is the strongest evidence a project can produce.","Very little, because every well shares the bandwidth, so the same bias runs the same way in all ten.","That the survey was processed consistently from one end of the field to the other, which is what the agreement demonstrates.","That the reflectivity was computed the same way in each well."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The error here is systematic rather than random, so it does not average out across wells and the usual cross-check returns a pass. Reading consistency as validation is the classic error under a shared bias.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-shift-workflow', 1, 'What is the practical form of the check that a bulk shift was found by a scan rather than by eye?', '["Ask whether the two traces were displayed at the same gain at the moment the shift was chosen, since gain is what drives an alignment made by eye.","Ask what the search range and the step were, because a shift nobody can state those for was not scanned.","Ask whether the shift is an even number of milliseconds.","Ask whether the shift was applied to the synthetic or to the observed trace."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A scanned shift has a stated range and step and can be rerun by anyone, which is what makes it a measurement rather than an opinion. An even shift is a consequence of the sample rate and would also come out of a lucky guess.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-shift-workflow', 2, 'Why is a bulk shift reported without its correlation only half a result?', '["Because the scan always returns some best lag.","Because a shift quoted in milliseconds cannot be checked by a reader who does not also know the sample rate of the grid it was measured on.","Because the shift means nothing until it has been applied to the trace.","Because the correlation is the field the capstone grades."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The scan will hand you an answer whatever you feed it, so the shift alone carries no evidence about the quality of the match. The last option is true of the capstone but is not the reason the pairing matters.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-shift-workflow', 3, 'A tie record quotes a correlation of 0.621742 and nothing else. What should you conclude?', '["That the tie is aligned, since a correlation above 0.6 between a synthetic and an observed trace is a strong match by any normal standard.","That the number proves very little, because 0.621742 is the correlation at zero lag and belongs to a tie sitting 8 ms wrong.","That the traces have opposite polarity.","That the scan failed to converge."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A plausible correlation is not evidence of alignment, which is the whole point of the zero-lag trap. Accepting 0.621742 on sight is the classic error of reading a single correlation without the curve it came from.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-shift-workflow', 4, 'Why is the correlation at the suggested shift exactly 1 in this capstone?', '["Because the normalised correlation is bounded above by 1 and the scan reports that bound whenever the two traces are a good match.","Because the synthetic and the observed trace were built from the same well and the same logs.","Because the observed trace is the 25 Hz synthetic copied forward by four samples.","Because the scan tests only lags at which the two traces overlap."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The exact 1 is a property of this exercise rather than of ties in general, since correlating a trace with a shifted copy of itself is an autocorrelation. Expecting a real tie to reach 1 is the misconception this field exists to expose.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-shift-workflow', 5, 'Why is the scan curve symmetric about the answer in this exercise?', '["Because it is an autocorrelation rather than a real tie.","Because the search range runs from minus 40 ms to plus 40 ms and is therefore symmetric about zero lag.","Because the normalised correlation is insensitive to the sign of the lag.","Because the Ricker wavelet is symmetric about its own peak."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The symmetry and the exact 1 have the same cause, which is that the observed trace is a copy of the synthetic. A symmetric search range is a property of the scan and would not make the curve symmetric on real data.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-shift-workflow', 6, 'What does the fourth quality-control check add over the third?', '["The third asks for the shift and the fourth asks for the correlation that goes with it.","The third applies to amplitudes and the fourth applies to picks made from them.","The third is run before the shift is applied to the trace and the fourth is run afterwards.","The third asks whether a frequency is present, the fourth whether it is the one the number was measured at."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A record can name a frequency and still be wrong if the number beside it came from a different run. The fourth check exists because a crossed pair passes the third one cleanly.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-shift-workflow', 7, 'What does the fifth quality-control check ask of a finished tie record?', '["Whether the synthetic was masked outside the logged interval before any statistic was computed over it.","Which of the two candidates the tie was hung on, a named contrast or the brightest event.","Whether the shift was applied in the correct direction.","Whether the reported amplitude exceeds the largest coefficient."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two candidates sit in different places at most frequencies, so the record has to say which one it used. A tie hung on brightness cannot be reproduced by anyone working at another bandwidth.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-shift-workflow', 8, 'The suggested bulk shift is graded in ms at a tolerance of 0.5. Why does that make the field effectively exact?', '["Because the planted lag is known to the engine, which checks the submission against it directly rather than against a window.","Because the correlation would fall below its own tolerance at any other lag in the scan.","Because the scan steps in one sample at a 2 ms rate, so only even milliseconds can be returned and the neighbours at 6 ms and 10 ms are nowhere near half a millisecond away.","Because the grader rounds every submission to the nearest whole millisecond."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The tolerance interacts with the sample rate, which is what leaves no near miss available. Nothing about the grading rounds a submission before comparing it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-shift-workflow', 9, 'How are the two graded amplitude fields treated, and why?', '["Both carry 0.002, because the two peaks are read from the same panel in the same way and deserve the same allowance.","The 15 Hz field carries 0.001 and the 40 Hz field carries 0.002, so the tighter window sits on the larger of the two values.","The 15 Hz field carries 0.002 and the 40 Hz field carries 0.001.","Both carry 0.001, because amplitudes are dimensionless."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two fields have different tolerances, and the smaller absolute window belongs to the smaller peak so that the grading is not far more forgiving there. The reversed option is the common transcription error.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-shift-workflow', 10, 'Both graded time fields carry a tolerance of 2 ms. What does that leave room for?', '["Almost nothing beyond the reading itself, since 2 ms is one sample on the grid and a peak taken from the wrong lobe or the wrong run misses by very much more than that.","A peak read at the neighbouring frequency, which lands within a sample of the right one.","A time quoted as one-way rather than two-way.","A time rounded to the nearest ten milliseconds."]'::jsonb, 0, 'One sample is the whole of the window, so the tolerance forgives the last digit and nothing structural. The second option is the crossed-pair error dressed up as a rounding allowance.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-shift-workflow', 11, 'How far can the two graded amplitudes be rounded before they stop passing?', '["Two decimal places clears both, since each tolerance is larger than 0.001 and two places is accurate to half of that.","Neither may be rounded at all, because the tolerances are small.","The 15 Hz field may be rounded to two places and the 40 Hz field to three.","Three decimal places clears both, and two fails both."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each rounding has to be tested against its own tolerance rather than against one rule for the pair. The safest habit is to hand in what the panel shows.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-shift-workflow', 12, 'What does the bulk-shift scan actually test on this exercise?', '["Every lag from minus 8 ms to plus 8 ms, since the planted lag is 8 ms and the scan is centred on the answer it expects to find.","Forty one lags from minus 40 ms to plus 40 ms in one-sample steps at 2 ms.","A continuous range of lags, reporting the maximum of a curve fitted through them.","The nine lags shown in the correlation table around the answer."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The range and the step together define what the scan can find, which is why the first quality-control check asks for both. The table around the answer is an excerpt of the scan rather than the scan itself.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-shift-workflow', 13, 'What are the four things a tie record produced at this tier has to contain?', '["The shift, the correlation it achieved, the wavelet frequency, and the feature tied to described in terms of the well.","The shift, the sample rate of the grid, the search range the scan used and the correlation it returned at the best lag.","The shift, the correlation, the peak amplitude and the peak time, all read once the two traces had been brought into alignment.","The shift, the wavelet frequency, the well name and the survey name."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The four are what let somebody else both reproduce the measurement and know what it was hung on. The option ending in the peak amplitude and time records the reading without recording the feature the tie was anchored to.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-shift-workflow', 14, 'What does this tier hand upward for the Expert tier to build on?', '["A reflectivity series that has been corrected for the wavelet used to build the synthetic.","A shift that was measured rather than eyeballed, and the habit of treating an amplitude as a joint statement about rock and bandwidth.","A wedge model and a tuning curve already computed for the teaching well.","An observed seismic trace with the planted lag removed from it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A resolution study built on a tie that is out by a wavelet cycle measures nothing, so the measured shift is the load-bearing hand-off. The wedge belongs to the tier above rather than to this one.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-shift-workflow', 15, 'The Expert tier runs its wedge on an equal and opposite reflection pair. Why that pairing?', '["Because a real bed always produces reflections of equal size and opposite sign at its top and at its base, so the model matches nature.","Because it makes the composite amplitude easier to normalise across thicknesses.","Because opposite signs are needed before the two reflections will interfere at all.","Because it isolates the interference, so any change as the bed thins comes from the two wavelet copies meeting."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Controlling one variable is what turns a model into a measurement, and here the controlled variable is the relative strength of the two reflections. Reflections of the same sign interfere as well, so opposite signs are a choice rather than a requirement.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'The scan hands you a suggested bulk shift of plus 8 ms. What do you do to the synthetic?', '["Subtract 8 ms from the two way time of every sample, which moves the trace earlier and brings it up the section to meet the observed event.","Add 8 ms to the observed seismic instead, since the synthetic is the reference trace and stays where it is.","Add 8 ms below the strongest event and leave the shallower part of the trace where it is.","Add 8 ms to the two way time of every sample, moving the whole trace later."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A positive shift means the synthetic is early, so it moves to larger two way time, which is downward on a conventional section. Reading the sign backwards is the classic error, and it is quiet because the display still looks plausible afterwards.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'On this exercise, what happens if a shift of minus 8 ms is applied instead of plus 8 ms?', '["The correlation falls to zero, which makes the mistake visible at once.","The synthetic moves 8 ms further from the observed data, leaving a 16 ms error where there was an 8 ms one, and nothing on the display announces it.","The scan refuses the shift because it lies on the wrong side of the window.","The tie is unchanged, because the correlation curve is symmetric."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Applying a shift in the wrong direction doubles the misfit rather than cancelling it. The trace still looks like a plausible synthetic sitting on plausible seismic, which is why the sign is worth deriving rather than guessing.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'You have applied a shift of minus 6 ms to a synthetic. How do you check that you moved it the right way?', '["Check that the strongest event on the synthetic now sits at the same two way time as the strongest reflection coefficient in the well, since a correct shift puts the two together.","Check that the correlation at the winning lag has risen above 0.621742.","Run the scan again on the shifted synthetic: a best lag near zero means you moved it correctly, and a best lag near minus 12 ms means you moved it the wrong way.","Check that the shift you applied is an even number of ms."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Rerunning the scan is the direct test, because a correction applied backwards leaves a residual of twice the original error. A rising correlation on its own does not settle the direction.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'Suppose an engine paired synthetic sample i with observed sample i minus the lag, with everything else about the scan unchanged. What would it report on this exercise?', '["Minus 8 ms, carrying the same physical instruction as plus 8 ms does here, which is to move the synthetic 8 ms later.","Minus 8 ms, meaning that the synthetic should be moved 8 ms earlier in two way time, since the sign of the reported number is the instruction.","Plus 8 ms, since the pairing rule affects only the internal arithmetic of the scan and never the sign that gets reported.","No answer, since the true lag would then fall outside the window."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The pairing rule and not the digit is what gives a lag its meaning, which is why a shift should always be recorded with the convention that produced it. Assuming the digit carries the instruction by itself is the classic sign error.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'Which of these values could the scan in this exercise never report as its suggested shift?', '["A shift of 8 ms, since that value is the planted lag and the engine is not told what it is.","7 ms.","A shift of 0 ms, since zero lag is always excluded from the set of candidates.","A shift of minus 40 ms, since the window covers only positive lags."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The scan steps in whole samples on a 2 ms grid, so every reported shift is an even number of ms. Zero lag is tested like any other lag, and the window covers minus 40 ms to plus 40 ms.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'Take a counterfactual in which the same 40 ms window is scanned on a 4 ms sample grid. How many lags are tested and what changes about the answer?', '["41 lags as before, since the window rather than the sample rate sets the count.","11 lags, since only positive lags need to be tested on a coarser grid.","21 lags, with the same precision as on the 2 ms grid.","21 lags in steps of 4 ms, and the reported shift can only be a multiple of 4 ms, so the answer is coarser than on the 2 ms grid."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Forty milliseconds is 10 samples at 4 ms, giving 10 negative lags, 10 positive lags and zero. Resolution follows the sample rate, so a coarser grid buys nothing in precision.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'Take a counterfactual in which a 100 ms window is scanned on the 2 ms grid used here. How many lags does the scan test?', '["101 lags.","201 lags, one for each millisecond in the window along with zero lag.","100 lags, being 50 negative lags and 50 positive lags.","51 lags, since the window is counted in one direction only."]'::jsonb, 0, 'One hundred milliseconds is 50 samples at 2 ms, so the scan runs from lag minus 50 to lag plus 50 in whole samples. That is 50 negative lags, 50 positive lags and zero lag itself.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'A scan run with a 40 ms window returns its best correlation at a lag of 40 ms. How should that result be treated?', '["As a confident answer, since that peak is the highest correlation found anywhere in the window and the search was exhaustive.","As a warning rather than a result, since the scan may only be reporting the closest it was allowed to look.","As proof that a stretch is present rather than a constant offset.","As a reason to narrow the window until the peak moves inside it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'An exhaustive scan is only exhaustive within its window, and a peak at the edge is the one case where the window itself is suspect. The honest response is to widen it and run the scan again.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'Which statement belongs in a defensible justification for a shift applied at a real well?', '["The correlation at the winning lag reached the value of 1 that a correctly aligned tie returns whenever the processing and the logs are both sound.","The correlation exceeded the fixed threshold that separates a good tie from a poor one, a threshold agreed for the whole survey beforehand.","The shift was chosen by eye and then confirmed on the display.","The peak stands clearly above the rest of the scanned range, it is sharp rather than flat, and its size matches a known datum discrepancy."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A correlation is a comparative instrument, so the evidence is the peak against its own curve together with the physical plausibility of the shift. Expecting a real tie to reach 1 is the classic error carried out of this exercise.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'An interpreter whose first real tie scores far below 1 keeps narrowing the correlation interval to the few events that already agree, and the score improves. What has happened?', '["The tie has genuinely improved, since a shorter interval contains fewer noisy samples.","The scan has become more precise, since fewer samples give a sharper peak.","The disagreement has been removed from the measurement rather than resolved, so the final number is better and the evidence behind it is weaker.","Nothing of consequence, since the shift itself did not change."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each step in that sequence is defensible on its own and the sequence is a slide into fitting the measure instead of tying the well. The protection is to know what your number means before going looking for a better one.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, 'Why is the correlation curve in this exercise symmetric about the winning lag, and why is a real scan curve usually not?', '["Because the Ricker wavelet is symmetric in time, which makes the correlation curve of any synthetic built with it symmetric about its own peak, whatever the second trace contains.","Sliding two identical shapes past each other looks the same from either side, and two genuinely different traces have no reason to behave that way.","Because the scan tests an equal number of negative and positive lags.","Because the sample rate is uniform across the trace."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The symmetry here is a property of comparing something with itself, which is what an autocorrelation is. An equal count of negative and positive lags sets the extent of the curve rather than its shape.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'In this scan a lag of zero, meaning a tie that was never shifted, scores 0.621742. What is the professional lesson?', '["A plausible correlation is not evidence of alignment.","A correlation above about 0.6 is enough to accept a tie on real data, since that is what an unshifted comparison already reaches here.","The scan should be started at zero lag and stopped once the correlation stops improving.","An unshifted tie is acceptable whenever its correlation is closer to 1 than to 0."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That value would look respectable to many people while the tie sits a full 8 ms wrong in two way time. Turning it into an acceptance threshold is the classic error, because it reads an absolute number as evidence about alignment.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'The scan tests every lag in its window rather than walking toward a solution from a starting guess. What does that guarantee?', '["That the answer it returns is the true time depth error at the well.","That the correlation at the winning lag is close to the maximum the measure allows.","That whatever it returns is genuinely the best of everything it looked at, so it cannot settle on a local peak and report success.","That the window was wide enough to contain the true answer."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Exhaustiveness removes one class of failure and leaves two others: the answer may lie outside the window, and the best correlation may not be the correct alignment. Neither of those is a failure of the search.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'The scan reports a shift and a correlation. Which of these does that pair leave unanswered?', '["Which lag agreed best among all those that were scored, since the engine reports only the winning value and discards the rest of the series.","How far the synthetic must move and in which direction along the two way time axis, since a bare lag on its own carries no sense of direction at all.","Whether the traces are inverted with respect to each other.","Whether the peak stands alone above a flat floor or sits at the top of a broad rise, and whether a constant shift was the right correction."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The winning pair carries no information about the shape of the rest of the curve, which is why the engine keeps the whole scored series. It also gives no warning when the true misfit grows with time, since it answers only the question it was asked.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'A tie report states a correlation and nothing else. What must it also carry before a reviewer can judge it?', '["The dominant frequency of the wavelet used to build the synthetic.","The number of live samples that overlapped at the reported lag.","The acoustic impedance of the strongest interface in the logged interval.","The lag that was applied and the fact that a window of lags was searched, so the reviewer can see that a comparison was made rather than a number produced."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A correlation on its own reports resemblance at one position, and only the searched window shows that the position beat its alternatives. Reporting the number alone is reporting half of the evidence.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'A scan on a new well returns its peak at a lag of 0 ms. How should you read that?', '["As confirmation that the time and depth relationship is correct, since a scan recommending no shift has found nothing to correct and the tie can be accepted as it stands.","As something to check further, since it is either a genuinely aligned tie or a scan that found nothing.","As a sign that the search window was too narrow and should be widened until a peak away from zero appears somewhere inside it.","As a failed scan, since a peak at zero lag cannot be distinguished from no result."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A peak at zero deserves a second look rather than relief, because the two cases that produce it are very different from each other. The first option is the classic error, taking a comfortable scan result as proof that the tie is sound.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'An upper window and a lower window on the same tie ask for different bulk shifts. What does that indicate?', '["That the correlation should be recomputed over the two windows combined, since a shift estimated from part of a trace is always less reliable than one estimated from all of it.","That the observed seismic has been polarity reversed in one of the two windows.","That the disagreement varies through the section, which is stretch or squeeze and cannot be corrected by any single constant shift.","That the wavelet frequency differs between the two windows."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A varying disagreement is exactly what a constant shift cannot express, so the two windows disagreeing is the direct evidence for a stretch or a squeeze. The first option is the classic error, folding the diagnostic back into the single number that hid the problem.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'After the recommended shift is applied, the events tie in the middle of the logged interval and drift apart at both ends. What has the scan done?', '["Split the difference of an interval error it cannot express.","Applied the shift with the wrong sign at one end of the logged interval.","Selected a lag from outside the window that was requested.","Aligned the synthetic to a reflector other than the one you had intended to tie."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Line up the top and the base is out, line up the base and the top is out, so the best constant shift lands in between and ties the middle. That signature belongs to stretch or squeeze rather than to a sign error or a mis-picked marker.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'You have learned the shape of a clean scan curve. What does a curve whose flanks disagree in shape tell you?', '["That the search window was not centred on zero, so the two sides of the curve were sampled over different ranges of lag and cannot be compared with each other.","That the two traces do not have the single clean relationship the symmetric case had, so the result deserves more scrutiny.","That the sample rate is too coarse to resolve the peak.","That the correlation was not normalised before the lags were compared."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Symmetry in the teaching case comes from correlating a trace against a shifted copy of itself, so flanks that disagree mean the pair is not related that cleanly. The window here runs from -40 ms to +40 ms and is centred on zero by construction.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'On the teaching pair, which description of what the scan achieved is accurate?', '["It found the correct lag and also showed that no nearby lag comes close to it.","It found the correct lag but could not say which lag was best.","It failed to find the correct lag because the shoulders are broad.","It determined the best lag exactly and left that lag only loosely constrained, since the neighbouring lags scored almost as well."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correctness and precision are separate: the peak is exact at 1.000000, and the neighbours at 0.972386 are close behind it. The third option is the classic error, reading a broad peak as a failure of the method.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'What connects the width of a scan peak to the subject of the amplitude module?', '["Nothing connects them, since the scan works on the observed seismic while the amplitude experiment works on the synthetic, so the two use different traces entirely.","Both depend on the reflection coefficients, since a densely layered interval produces a sharper peak and a larger amplitude at every frequency used.","Both depend on wavelet length, since a long wavelet makes a trace resemble itself over a wider range of lags and also reaches across more coefficients.","Both depend on the sample rate of 2 ms."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Wavelet length is the common cause: it broadens the scan peak and it widens the reach that lets neighbouring coefficients sum. Sharper wavelets produce sharper peaks because a trace of short pulses stops resembling itself as soon as you slide it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'Two surveys are merged and a horizon is mapped across the join. What condition must hold before amplitudes on either side can be compared as rock properties?', '["The wavelets in the two volumes must be comparable, and that is a condition to be checked rather than assumed, because a difference in wavelet alone moves amplitudes by a large factor.","The horizon must have been picked on the same phase in both volumes.","The two surveys must share the same time and depth relationship.","The two surveys must have been acquired in the same year."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Amplitude is a property of the reflectivity and the wavelet together, so it is not portable between volumes whose wavelets differ. Picking on the same phase keeps the measurement consistent and does nothing about the underlying bandwidth difference.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'What is an amplitude quoted without saying which wavelet produced it?', '["A complete result, since the amplitude is what was measured and the wavelet is only context.","Half a result, since the pair produced it.","An error, because amplitudes cannot be quoted numerically at all.","A statement about the rock, provided the volume was processed to zero phase."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Reflectivity and wavelet together produce an amplitude, so naming only the number leaves out one of the two ingredients. The last option is the classic error, treating a processing property as though it removed the wavelet from the result.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'A colleague builds a synthetic at a low frequency, sees a large peak, and concludes that the reservoir is a strong reflector. Where is the flaw?', '["The peak grew because a longer wavelet gathered more neighbouring reflections into the sum, and no reflection coefficient changed when the frequency was lowered.","The peak grew because low frequency energy penetrates further and returns more of itself to the receiver, so the amplitude is a depth effect rather than a reservoir property.","The synthetic should have been built at the frequency of the seismic, so the peak is at the wrong time.","Low frequency synthetics cannot be used for amplitude work of any kind."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The brightness is bought by blurring, and blurring is not strength, since the reflectivity was untouched. The classic error here is inferring reflector strength from amplitude when the only thing that changed was the wavelet.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'Why is the three run experiment on this well a stronger argument than the same observation made across two different surveys?', '["Because a synthetic is computed rather than recorded, so it contains no noise, and a noiseless result is more reliable than any measurement made on field data.","Because three points always establish a trend, while an observation made on two surveys can only ever establish a difference between two numbers.","Because the capstone grades two of the three runs.","Because exactly one input varied while everything else was held fixed, so the change in the output has only one possible cause."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Isolating one variable is what no real dataset lets you do, and it is what removes every competing explanation for the change. Two surveys differ in acquisition, processing and geology at once, so nothing there can be attributed cleanly.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'At the Associate tier you met constructive interference. What does the choice of dominant frequency control about it?', '["Whether it happens at all, since interference requires a wavelet above a threshold frequency and below that threshold the reflections stay independent of each other.","The sign of each contribution, since a higher frequency wavelet flips the polarity of alternate coefficients.","How wide a window contributions have in which to find each other.","The size of each reflection coefficient before the copies are summed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Frequency sets wavelet length, and length sets how easy it is for same signed contributions to coincide, so a long wavelet makes reinforcement common. Signs and coefficient sizes come from the rock and are untouched by the wavelet.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'Which item in the tie workflow belongs to the rock alone?', '["The reflectivity series, because every step that produced it, from sonic and density to impedance and contrast, is a measurement of the formation.","The synthetic trace, because it is built from logs rather than from field seismic.","The correlation at the best lag, because it is computed from data at both ends.","The wavelet, because it represents what the earth returned."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The synthetic is the reflectivity convolved with a wavelet you chose, so it carries a modelling choice inside it and the reflectivity does not. That is why the reflectivity is the reference a bright event has to be checked against.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'You are asked for a one sentence rule that a manager who is not a geophysicist can act on. Which sentence is both true and actionable?', '["Brightness ranks prospects reliably as long as every prospect is measured on the same horizon and in the same time window, because those two conditions remove the effect of the wavelet.","Amplitudes must not be compared across datasets of different bandwidth, and brightness alone does not rank prospects.","Low frequency data should always be preferred for interpretation work, since it consistently shows the larger amplitudes of the two.","High frequency data should always be preferred whenever the work involves reading amplitude values off a seismic section."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rule states the limit on comparison and the limit on ranking, both of which follow from an amplitude being a property of the reflectivity and the wavelet together. The third option is the classic error in its most tempting form, treating a larger number as a better result when the brightness was bought by blurring.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'Why does the reading discipline of this module have to be automatic rather than something you apply when it seems to matter?', '["Because the panel does not retain a previous reading once the frequency control has been moved to a new value, so the earlier pair is gone.","Because the engine recomputes the reflectivity at each frequency and the earlier reading goes stale.","Because none of these failures produces a display that looks wrong or a value out of range, so what they produce is a clean report.","Because the capstone is timed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The failures are silent, which removes the moment at which you would otherwise notice and go back. The second option is wrong because the reflectivity is fixed before the wavelet is chosen.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'What is the practical test of whether your own pick is anchored properly?', '["Ask whether a colleague rebuilding the synthetic from the same logs at a different bandwidth would land on the same feature, which they would if the pick is anchored to a coefficient and might not if it is anchored to brightness.","Ask whether the pick agrees with the seismic horizon already carried in the project database.","Ask whether the pick was made after the bulk shift had been applied.","Ask whether the pick sits inside the logged interval."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Reproducibility across bandwidths is what separates a pick that belongs to the well from one that belongs to a display. The last option is a necessary condition but tests nothing about the anchor.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'The rule that a wavelet frequency belongs in the same line as the number it describes is compared to which existing convention?', '["Quoting a correlation together with the search range and step that produced it.","Quoting an amplitude as an absolute value rather than as a signed one.","Quoting a time as two-way rather than one-way.","Quoting a depth with its unit."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The defect is the same in both cases, which is a number that looks complete while carrying no way to interpret it. The two-way convention is a rule about what the number means rather than about how it is labelled.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'Why does a tuning-driven pick error not average out over many wells the way a random error would?', '["Because pick errors always run in the same direction as the bulk shift that was applied before them, so the two accumulate.","Because everyone working a volume works at its bandwidth, so every well carries the same bias in the same direction.","Because the wells are not independent measurements of the same surface.","Because there are rarely enough wells for averaging to help."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A random error moves around a true value and partly cancels across many measurements, while a shared bandwidth produces one bias repeated. The last option is a practical complaint rather than the reason.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'A pick displaced by tuning is carried into the rest of the work. Which sequence describes how it travels?', '["Into the horizon picked from the tie, then the structure map gridded from it, then the depth conversion and the prognosis, then any isochore built between two horizons, and finally the volume computed inside the closure.","Into the bulk shift and then into the correlation that is reported alongside it.","Into the reflectivity series and then into the impedance log behind it.","Into the wavelet and then into every other well tied with it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each product consumes the one before it without rechecking the tie, which is why the original decision is several steps back by the time anyone notices. The third option runs the pipeline backwards, since nothing downstream of the tie can change the reflectivity.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'A reprocessed volume disagrees with a set of ties that were anchored to the brightest event. What is actually wrong?', '["The reprocessing has introduced a static shift that has to be measured and removed before the old ties can be used again.","The reflectivity has to be recomputed against the new volume before the old ties will agree with the reprocessed data.","Nothing is wrong with the ties as measurements, since they were measurements of the old bandwidth all along.","The wells have been remapped onto a new datum."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The natural assumption when a tie stops working is that the tie is broken, and the time lost is spent hunting for a fault that was never there. The second option is the classic error of thinking reflectivity depends on the survey.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'How does the 66 ms swing in the peak time compare with the precision the capstone demands when you report one peak time?', '["It is about three times the tolerance, which is why the two time fields are graded more loosely than the amplitudes.","It is sixteen times the tolerance, one for each sample of the wavelet.","It is about the same as the tolerance, within a sample.","It is thirty three times the 2 ms tolerance."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A quantity you must report to within one sample is being relocated by thirty three samples by a decision that is usually not written down anywhere. That mismatch between the precision demanded and the effect ignored is the point of the comparison.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'Why is it reasonable that the peak relocates by something on the order of 66 ms rather than by an arbitrary amount?', '["Because the logged interval is 150 ms long and the peak cannot leave it, which caps how far any relocation can reach on this particular well.","Because the peak moves when a different group of coefficients falls inside the reach of the wavelet, so the wavelet length sets the scale.","Because the sample rate of 2 ms quantises the movement into steps.","Because the scan range is 40 ms on each side of zero lag."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The mechanism bounds the effect, which is what makes it predictable in size even though it is not predictable in direction. The logged interval limits where a reading can be taken but does not explain the scale of the movement.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'Why should a reported peak be accompanied by its separation from the coefficient it is supposed to represent?', '["Because the separation is constant across frequencies and therefore travels with the well rather than with the survey.","Because the separation is the quantity the bulk shift has to remove before the tie can be recorded.","Because a reader cannot compute the separation from a peak reading alone.","Because the capstone grades the separation as a seventh field."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The separation is a quality statement about the peak that the peak itself does not carry. The first option is wrong, since the separation is about 2 ms at 15 Hz and about 60 ms at the other two frequencies on this well.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'Two interpreters follow the same instruction to tie on the strongest event and end up 66 ms apart on the same well. What can either of them see from inside their own work?', '["Nothing at all, because each has a clean synthetic beside a clean section with a bright event lining up on a bright event, and the disagreement appears only when the two ties meet in one project.","A correlation that has fallen below the value they expected from the scan.","A peak amplitude that does not match the strongest coefficient.","A gap where the wavelet ran off the logged interval."]'::jsonb, 0, 'There is no artefact, no null and no unit out of place, which is why the failure survives every check made on a single workstation. The absence of a visible symptom is what makes recording the wavelet necessary.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'What condition must a lag meet before the scan will score it at all?', '["It must leave behind at least eight overlapping samples that are live rather than gaps.","It must produce a correlation above zero at that lag before the scan will consider it at all.","It must lie inside the logged interval of the well, which is the only part the panel treats as live.","It must be an even number of milliseconds."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The scan refuses to score a lag it cannot support with enough real data, which prevents a spurious high correlation from a handful of samples. Being an even number of milliseconds is a consequence of the sample rate rather than a condition the scan applies.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'Comparing the two graded amplitude fields in relative terms rather than absolute terms, which is the tighter?', '["The 40 Hz field, because its absolute window of 0.001 is the smaller of the two and a smaller window is a tighter one.","The 15 Hz field, at 1.3 percent against 2.8 percent.","Neither, because the two windows are proportional to the values they sit on.","They cannot be compared, because amplitudes are dimensionless."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The smaller absolute window sits on the smaller value, which reverses the ranking once each is expressed as a fraction of the value it grades. Reading the absolute windows as the whole story is the error the first option embodies.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'How many panel states does the capstone actually require, and in what order?', '["Three, which are the scan first, then the wavelet at 15 Hz for the amplitude and the time taken as a pair, then the wavelet at 40 Hz for the second pair.","Six, one for each of the graded fields, read in the order the capstone lists them.","Two, one for each of the two graded frequencies.","One, since the panel reports every field at once."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Taking each amplitude with its own time in one panel state is what prevents the four readings from being assembled out of notes afterwards. Six states would mean reading each field separately, which reintroduces the crossing risk.'),
  ('seismolord', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'What is the second thing the Expert tier adds, beyond the wedge?', '["A bulk-shift scan that searches a considerably wider range of lags than the one used at this tier.","A second wavelet family to compare against the Ricker used throughout this course.","It follows a fluid-substituted sand into AVO screening.","A campaign view that ties several wells at once."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every reflection coefficient in this course has been the normal incidence case, and AVO replaces that single number with a behaviour whose shape carries fluid information. The other options describe extensions that belong to neither tier.');
